WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.408 --> 00:00:02.200
STEPHANIE WONG: Hi.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.590
My name is Stephanie
Wong, and today, we're

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.080
going to explore how
BigQuery, Google Cloud's fully

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.760
managed enterprise
data warehouse,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.030
enables you to run analytics
at scale, using standard SQL

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.830
with zero operational overhead.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.570
It provides unique
functionality,

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.480
like realtime stream
ingestion, query federation,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.840
GIS functions, and built-in
machine learning capabilities.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.460
And to bring this
to life, let's look

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.110
at how New Kicks,
a fictional brick

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.260
and mortar and online retailer,
uses advanced analytics

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.073
and BigQuery to optimize
their supply chain.

00:00:31.073 --> 00:00:33.240
The challenge started when
the director of marketing

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.000
wanted to make better
ad targeting decisions.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.340
The task was given to the
lead data scientist and data

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.800
engineer, and they recognized
four challenges to overcome.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.860
First up was to break down
the various data silos

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.720
within their organization.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.780
The data engineer at New Kicks
built out a one-to-end mapping

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.020
of key data sources, including
Google Analytics, raw click

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.380
stream, online transactional,
and in-store inventory data

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.720
that all move data
into BigQuery.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.630
Store operations data is stored
in the transactional database,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.070
Cloud SQL.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.349
These data sources were
ingested via combination

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:07.350
of BigQuery data transfer
service and Cloud Dataflow

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.280
in batch and streaming mode.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.933
Let's take a look
at this in action.

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:13.350
This is the Cloud
Dataflow console

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.590
showing New Kicks' real-time
ETL for in-store transactions,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.840
stock levels, and
click stream data.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.960
This job ran tandem with
batch and data transfer jobs

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.800
to hydrate various data
sets in the data warehouse.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.300
The click stream step was
processing around 200 events

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.570
per second, with BigQuery
as a target output.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.210
This data was immediately
available for querying.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.850
For example, this query shows
the most recent click stream

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.570
events.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.570
Note the latest event is about
two seconds behind current UTC

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.130
time.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.360
With real-time access
to multiple data sets,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.690
they could perform more
meaningful analysis,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.080
like this query joining online
sales with in-store sales data,

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.600
calculating ranks for
the top 10 sneakers.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.730
Accessibility across
various warehouses

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.890
makes the data more actionable.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.980
However, after reviewing current
schemas in the data warehouse,

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.870
the data scientist
and data engineer

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.480
realized they needed to perform
some additional and possibly

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.330
heavy transformation and
enrichment, in order to get

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.770
the right data sets in place.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.020
And they were going to need
some additional processing

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.730
resources to get the job done.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.870
Now, to drive key data warehouse
workloads like ingestion, ETL,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.850
and business
intelligence, New Kicks

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.300
used a combination of
BigQuery pricing models--

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.790
on-demand and flat rate-- to
optimize resources and cost.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.490
For lower query volume
workloads like query prototyping

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.890
and exploratory
analysis, the IT manager

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.250
utilized on-demand pricing with
project-level cost controls.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.310
However, the forecasting project
presented new challenges,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.110
like unknown query
volume with the need

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.390
to minimize disruption to
existing production workloads.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.410
In addition, resources were
only needed for a few days.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.540
This is where the power
and value of flex slots

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.070
came into play.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.080
Flex slots let you
purchase BigQuery capacity

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.440
for short durations-- as
little as 60 seconds at a time.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.630
Using the BigQuery UI
or the client libraries,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.810
the IT manager was able to
immediately allocate 500 flex

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.450
slots to a new project that
the data scientists and data

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.645
engineer could work in.

00:03:02.645 --> 00:03:04.020
The serverless
nature of BigQuery

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.120
enables deploying this
additional capacity

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.678
in a few seconds, and can be
shut down after 60 seconds,

00:03:09.678 --> 00:03:11.970
so it's an excellent solution
for short-term and bursty

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.590
workloads.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.212
With their additional and
isolated capacity in place,

00:03:16.212 --> 00:03:18.420
the team started to make
quick work on transformation

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.490
and enrichment.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.320
To drive the forecasting
model, the team

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.960
needed to produce a filtered
and enriched click stream that

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.700
had several key
attributes, including

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.670
customer location, specific
target action, and store

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:30.840
location.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.880
This required a series
of transformations,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.830
including filtering,
joining, and applying

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.350
GIS functions, all of
which can be accomplished

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.210
at scale in BigQuery.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.700
To perform these
transformations,

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.420
New Kicks' data
scientists executed

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.540
a series of query jobs inside
of AI platform, Notebooks,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.880
with streamlined
integration with BigQuery.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.800
The goal here was to
produce a filtered click

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.260
stream that was joined to
customer and in-store pickup

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.190
data.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.870
The first query job
filtered out clicks

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.970
that meet a specific
requirement, including

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.040
active sessions that
ended with a purchase,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.870
and an in-store pickup.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.960
This query reduces the
analysis set from 10 terabytes

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.545
to 1 terabyte.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.170
Notice that the
results were saved back

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.270
as a new destination table.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.950
Next, the customer
table was enriched

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.080
with geocoordinates,
which were joined back

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.520
to the click stream.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.500
She then joined click
stream to orders.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.810
To obtain the geo
information for a store,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.850
she used an external
query function

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.220
to execute a query into
the store operations

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.740
database, located on Cloud SQL.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.830
This type of query federation
reduces data copying.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.050
With geo information for
customers and stores,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.780
she applied one final
transformation and filter.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.328
Using built-in
GIS functions, she

00:04:35.328 --> 00:04:37.620
could calculate the distance
between customer residence

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.210
and store location.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.070
Built-in GIS
functions enable you

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.530
to project analysis
in-line without having

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.240
to export your data.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.690
This query produces candidate
feature inputs for the model.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.430
If she wants to
audit this process,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.810
she can walk back through
the tables created.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.800
Over time, these queries could
become materialized views

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.930
and/or stream or
batch transforms,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.750
setting the foundation
for a feature store.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.320
With the transformed
data in place,

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.240
they were able to
move onto modeling,

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.730
so they turned to BigQuery ML.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.722
If you have experience
with machine learning,

00:05:08.722 --> 00:05:10.180
you know that
traditional workflows

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:13.060
require additional
pre-processing, data splitting,

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.490
model development,
and deployment steps.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.220
But with BigQuery ML,
steps like pre-processing,

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.620
model development, and
deployment are removed.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.450
It lets you build
machine learning models

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.580
using standard SQL,
and without the need

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.060
to move your data for external
training and evaluation.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.250
First, New Kicks' data
scientist and a quick sample

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.990
of the transformed
click stream data.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.170
For this model, she wanted to
look at top line, all products,

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.600
per day, click stream
volume, so they needed

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.580
to apply a quick
transformation to group

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:41.050
all of the clicks by day.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.280
No problem.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.110
With BigQuery ML,
the data scientists

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.730
can create various model
types, including regression,

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.550
clustering, and XGBoost.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.380
In this case, she
used ARIMA, which

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.553
is built to forecast future
time series-based values.

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:54.970
Here, she told the
model what data

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.670
target to forecast and
time series columns to use,

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.550
and provided a hint
regarding US-based holidays.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:03.040
Finally, she passed in
the query of click streams

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.710
that looks back two years but
prunes out the last 30 days.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.740
This model trains on
two years of data,

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.550
and then saves into the data
set for evaluation and serving.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.590
She then used the
ML Forecast option

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.080
to forecast the next 30
days in the time series,

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.510
at a 90% confidence interval.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:22.240
To verify the model estimates,
she selected the actuals

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.010
from the 30-day window.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:27.220
From there, she could plot
the historical plus forecast

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:30.220
plus actual and the lower and
upper bound of the confidence

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.900
intervals.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.910
This graph passes
a quick eye test.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.370
You can see seasonality
changes over quarter,

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.200
and then surges
during the weekends.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.790
Once we get into
the forecast, you

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.820
can see that the model is within
three of the four week windows,

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:46.780
and barely misses week 1, even
though it saw an upward trend.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.550
With an end-to-end
workflow in place,

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.070
the data scientists can go
back into additional EDA

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.250
for outliers and errors, and
then persist these results back

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.970
to the warehouse and/or
share the model for serving.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.250
With a top line model
in place, New Kicks

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.980
can move on to additional models
like per-store and per-product

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.360
based forecasting.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.160
Problem solved.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.860
In this demo, we showed you how
New Kicks efficiently extended

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.680
a BigQuery data warehouse to
create a demand forecasting

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.020
model by utilizing various
transformation patterns, flex

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:19.930
slots, federated queries, GIS,
and machine learning functions.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.120
Check out these links to
learn more, and get started

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.180
with building your cloud native
data warehouse on Google Cloud.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:29.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

